BEGIN:VEVENT
SUMMARY:New Players\, Old Plays? Continuity and Change in Development Dipl
 omacy — Washington\, DC
DTSTART;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261120T120000
DTEND;TZID="UTC-05:00":20261120T130000
DTSTAMP:20261121T192054Z
UID:c86f8a587f8e25561db509b0c460@global-arts-culture
CATEGORIES:Arts & Culture\,Lectures & Speakers\,Washington\, DC\,United St
 ates
DESCRIPTION:Overview Are emerging powers reshaping international developme
 nt cooperation or playing by the existing rules? Abstract The growing part
 icipation of emerging powers in international development cooperation rais
 es questions about the changing nature of development diplomacy. As these 
 actors expand their roles as donors\, are they challenging established pra
 ctices and structures\, or reproducing them? More broadly\, does the arriv
 al of new actors necessarily change how development diplomacy is practiced
  and understood? This talk explores these questions through two ongoing re
 search projects that examine the structural and communicative dimensions o
 f development diplomacy. The first study\, Claimin\nCategory: Lectures & S
 peakers\nOriginal listing: https://calendar.gwu.edu/event/new-players-old-
 plays-continuity-and-change-in-development-diplomacy
LOCATION:School of Media and Public Affairs\, 805 21st St NW\, Washington
 \, DC 20052
